# Minimal helper image carrying nft - and the tools to build a WireGuard interface - for the
# privileged init steps that run in the app pod's network namespace before the app
# (docs/architecture.md 5.3). It runs once per launch inside the app pod's network
# namespace (with namespaced CAP_NET_ADMIN), loads the ruleset zcr pipes in on
# stdin, and exits - the rules persist in the pod netns for the app that starts
# next, so the app never sees an open network.
#
# Local by design (section 5.5): referenced by a local tag (zinc/netfilter:local)
# and run with --pull never, built from this vetted Containerfile whose FROM base
# is pinned by digest. Unlike the reproducible Go-tool build, this one runs `apk
# add` and so needs network at build time - acceptable for a locally built helper.
# nft is pinned by version below.
#
# Build:  make -C container/runner netfilter-image
FROM docker.io/library/alpine@sha256:4bcff63911fcb4448bd4fdacec207030997caf25e9bea4045fa6c8c44de311d1

# Pinned to the versions in the pinned base's repo (alpine v3.22/main) for
# reproducibility - this image runs with CAP_NET_ADMIN and writes the egress
# ruleset, so two builds of one commit must not produce different `nft`, `ip` or `wg`.
# ALL FOUR top-level packages carry a version; apk resolves the dep tree below them, so
# transitive versions still follow the branch. Note the standing cost: Alpine deletes
# superseded -rN builds from a branch, so these pins eventually stop resolving and need a
# deliberate refresh, exactly like the Go image digest.
# iproute2 and wireguard-tools are here for the tunnel step: Zinc creates and configures the
# app's WireGuard interface itself, in this helper, so the APP never needs CAP_NET_ADMIN -
# which it may not have anyway, since NET_ADMIN in the pod netns would let it flush the very
# ruleset that contains it. busybox's `ip` cannot create a wireguard link, hence iproute2.
#
# xdg-dbus-proxy is the D-Bus half of this image's job (DBusMeta). Unlike the nft and
# wireguard steps it does NOT exit before the app starts: it runs for the app's whole life,
# in its own container, serving the filtered socket. It lives here rather than in a second
# image because it is the same kind of thing - a vetted helper Zinc drives, never the app's
# own image - and one helper image is one thing to build and keep pinned.
RUN apk add --no-cache nftables=1.1.3-r0 iproute2=6.15.0-r0 wireguard-tools=1.0.20250521-r0 xdg-dbus-proxy=0.1.6-r0

# No ENTRYPOINT: zcr invokes `nft -f -` explicitly (the netenforce adapter builds
# the apply args).
